.\" ai-tools-secret-patterns(5) -- an operator's secret-name patterns for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The matcher described here is the one
.\" secret-patterns.lib.sh implements for every helper that classifies a name.
.TH AI-TOOLS-SECRET-PATTERNS 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-secret-patterns \- the file names an operator's sandbox treats as credentials
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each operator keeps one
.B secret-patterns
file.
A path whose base name matches a pattern in it is a credential file, and the
sandbox keeps it out of the agent's reach:
.TP
.B on write
A secret-named file the agent creates is quarantined the moment it is written:
owned by you at mode
.BR 0600 ,
which the agent can neither read nor edit in place.
The session receives a notice naming the path.
.TP
.B on claim, and on demand
.B "atr projects lockdown"
walks a project and seals every secret-named file already in it the same way,
before the agent is granted access.
.B "atr projects claim"
and
.B "atr projects clone"
run that scan first.
.PP
The root helpers read this file on your behalf.
It is owned by you at mode
.B 0600
inside a
.B 0700
directory, so the sandbox account can neither read it nor change what it
classifies.
.PP
.B "atr admin operators add"
creates it with a header and no pattern, which leaves the built-in baseline in
force (see
.BR "THE BASELINE" ).
.SH GRAMMAR
One pattern per line.
A pattern is a glob matched against a file's
.B base name
only, so it does not contain a
.BR / .
Matching is case-insensitive:
.B .env
also matches
.BR .ENV .
.TP
.B *
matches any run of characters, including none.
.TP
.B ?
matches one character.
.TP
.BI [ SET ]
matches one character from the set.
.TP
.BI @( A | B )
matches one of the alternatives.
.TP
.BI ?( A | B )
matches one of the alternatives, or nothing.
.TP
.BI !( A | B )
matches any run of characters that none of the alternatives matches.
.PP
These are bash's extended globs, read whether or not the shell's
.B \%extglob
option is set.
A
.B .
is literal.
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment; blank lines and surrounding whitespace are ignored.
.PP
A line beginning with
.B !
and any character but
.B (
is an exemption, not a pattern:
.B !
and one absolute path keep that path open whatever pattern matches its name.
The path is compared byte for byte with its canonical form, the one
.B realpath
prints, so it does not take a glob character, a
.B .
or
.B ..
component, or a doubled or trailing
.BR / .
A line that does not name such a path is ignored and reported, and the path it
meant stays sealed.
An exemption covers the path it names and not a path under it, and it names
the path, not its content: the path stays open whatever the file later holds.
A file holding only exemption lines and comments keeps the baseline in force.
.SH THE BASELINE
The shared library
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/secret-patterns.lib.sh
carries a built-in list: the names credential files carry across software in
general, kept current by package upgrades.
.PP
.B "A pattern listed in this file replaces that baseline rather than adding to it."
A file holding one pattern classifies on that pattern alone.
To add a name of your own, copy the baseline entries you keep from the library
and write your own beside them.
.PP
When this file is missing, or does not hold a pattern line, the baseline is in
force,
so a fresh enrolment quarantines every name the baseline lists and every name a
later upgrade adds.
A file that is present and cannot be read as a regular file (a directory in
its place, a dangling symlink) is refused: a claim, a handback, an unclaim
and a lockdown each stop before changing a path and name the file, and the
baseline classifies until it is a readable file again or removed.
A file you have written keeps this host on the patterns it listed then, without
the baseline's path exemptions; at each launch the wrapper logs how the set in
force differs from the baseline, naming the patterns it drops, or that a copy of
the baseline drops the exemptions, so a stale copy is visible in the journal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
journalctl -t claude -g 'secret patterns'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Anchor a pattern to a name or to an environment segment, as the baseline does
.RB ( appsettings.*.json ,
.BR web.*.config ).
The baseline's environment-segment patterns leave a script, a stylesheet and a
source map alone, so a vendored bundle such as
.B \%react.production.min.js
stays readable and
.B \%appsettings.Production.json
is quarantined:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
*.Production.!(?(*.)@(js|mjs|cjs|css|map))
.EE
.RE
.PP
A broad catch-all such as
.B *.*.json
also matches the build artifacts a toolchain must read, and a quarantined
artifact breaks the build.
A pattern matches any file named after its subject as well: a document called
.B secrets.md
matches
.B secrets.*
and becomes unreadable to the agent.
.PP
The baseline also leaves open the paths a package or a project generator ships
under a name it matches: the
.B private
and
.B tokens
source directories of Angular's build and Material packages, the library
template of its schematics, and the environment files its application template
writes, such as
.BR \%src/environments/environment.prod.ts .
Each is matched by its last path components, case-sensitively, wherever the
project or the package manager places it, and a file or directory under it is
classified by its own name.
The list is
.B \%_AI_TOOLS_SECRET_PATTERNS__BASELINE_EXEMPT_PATHS
in the library.
A file with a pattern line replaces these exemptions with the baseline, and
keeps a path open with a
.B !
line.
.PP
A credential name that software writes in general, and that the baseline lacks,
belongs upstream, so every host receives it on the next upgrade.
The library is replaced on upgrade, so an edit made there is lost.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
This file, one per operator.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
The operator's project registry, kept beside it with the same ownership.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/secret-patterns.lib.sh
The shared matcher and the built-in baseline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Quarantine a deployment-specific name and keep three baseline entries:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# names the agent must not read
deploy-*.yaml
\&.env
*.pem
appsettings.*.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep one file open, after reading it and judging its content safe for the agent
to read, with the baseline in force:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
!/home/op/src/api/appsettings.Development.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Review what a project holds before granting access, then seal it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr projects lockdown \-\-dry\-run /home/op/webapp
atr projects lockdown /home/op/webapp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
.BR ai\-tools\-project\-registry (5),
.BR glob (7)
